---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { features, limits, site } from '../config/site';
---

<PageLayout
  title="How Our Image Tools Work – Formats, Methods & Limits"
  heading="How it works"
  description={`How ${site.name} processes images in your browser: the decoders and encoders used, supported formats, quality settings, limits, and how to verify nothing is uploaded.`}
  path="/methodology"
  lead="Exactly what happens to your files, which technologies we use, and where the limits are."
>
  <h2 id="local">1. Processing happens on your device</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} is a static website. When you choose a file, the browser reads it from your device into memory. All decoding, editing and encoding runs as JavaScript and WebAssembly inside that browser tab — mostly in a background <em>Web Worker</em> so the page stays responsive. The finished file is created in memory and handed to your browser’s normal download function.
  </p>
  <p>There is no server-side processing, no upload endpoint and no storage of files. When you close or reload the tab, the files are released from memory.</p>

  <h2 id="verify">2. How you can verify there is no upload</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open your browser’s developer tools (F12, or right-click → Inspect) and switch to the <strong>Network</strong> tab.</li>
    <li>Use any tool with a file.</li>
    <li>You will see requests for the page’s own code (for example the WebP encoder or PDF library, which are downloaded <em>to</em> your browser the first time they are needed) but no request that sends your file anywhere.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    As an extra safeguard, every page is served with a <strong>Content Security Policy</strong> whose <code>connect-src</code> directive only allows connections to this site itself (plus the optional, cookieless Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon, which receives page-view data only). Even a bug could not send a file to a third-party server. Form submissions are disabled entirely with <code>form-action 'none'</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="detection">3. File detection and safety checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format detection by content.</strong> The first bytes of each file (its “magic number”) decide the format. Extensions and the MIME type reported by the operating system are ignored, so a renamed file cannot pretend to be an image.</li>
    <li><strong>Size and dimension limits before decoding.</strong> Files larger than {limits.maxFileBytes / 1024 / 1024} MB are rejected. For JPG, PNG, GIF, WebP, BMP and AVIF, the pixel dimensions are read from the header first; images over {limits.maxInputPixels / 1e6} megapixels (less on devices that report 4 GB of RAM or less) are rejected before the browser allocates memory (protection against “decompression bombs”).</li>
    <li><strong>No SVG input.</strong> SVG files can contain scripts and external references, so they are not accepted by the raster tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe file names.</strong> Output names are derived from your file name with path separators, control characters, invisible bidirectional characters and reserved names removed. The extension is always set by us from the real output format.</li>
    <li><strong>Batch limits.</strong> Up to {limits.maxBatchFiles} files per batch. ZIP downloads are flat (no folders) with de-duplicated names.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="decoders">4. Decoders</h2>
  <table class="spec-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">JPG, PNG, WebP, GIF, BMP, AVIF</th><td>The browser’s built-in decoder (<code>createImageBitmap</code>). EXIF orientation is applied, so photos appear upright. Animated images use the first frame.</td></tr>
      {features.heicDecoder ? (<tr><th scope="row">HEIC / HEIF</th><td>The browser/OS decoder when available (Safari on macOS/iOS). Otherwise <a href="https://github.com/strukturag/libheif" rel="noopener">libheif</a> via the open-source <code>heic-to</code> package (LGPL-3.0), compiled to JavaScript and loaded on demand.</td></tr>) : (<tr><th scope="row">HEIC / HEIF</th><td>Not supported in this version. The EXIF Viewer can still read metadata from HEIC files, because that does not require decoding the image.</td></tr>)}
      <tr><th scope="row">TIFF</th><td>Only where the browser supports it natively (Safari). Used by the Image Converter and EXIF Viewer.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="encoders">5. Encoders and quality</h2>
  <table class="spec-table">
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">JPG</th><td>Browser encoder via <code>OffscreenCanvas.convertToBlob</code>/<code>canvas.toBlob</code>. Quality is the value you choose (tool defaults: 75–92%). Transparent areas are filled with a solid colour (white by default).</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">WebP</th><td>Browser encoder where supported. Safari and all iOS browsers cannot encode WebP and silently return PNG instead — we detect this and use Google’s libwebp compiled to WebAssembly (<code>@jsquash/webp</code>). Lossless WebP always uses libwebp.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">PNG</th><td>Lossless: browser encoder. Lossy (Image Compressor): colour quantization to a 32–256 colour palette with UPNG.js, which keeps transparency.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">PDF</th><td><code>pdf-lib</code>. JPG files without a rotation flag are embedded byte-for-byte (no recompression); PNG is embedded losslessly; other formats are converted to JPG at 92% first.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="resizing">6. Resizing</h2>
  <p>
    When an image is reduced by more than half, it is first halved repeatedly and then scaled to the final size with the browser’s high-quality smoothing. This avoids the aliasing (jagged edges and moiré) of a single large reduction. Enlarging is possible in the Image Resizer but cannot add detail.
  </p>

  <h2 id="target-size">7. Compressing to a target size</h2>
  <p>
    The “Compress to exact KB” tool treats 1 KB as 1,000 bytes, so results pass forms that use either 1,000 or 1,024. It encodes at 92% quality first; if the file is too big it performs a binary search over quality between 40% and 92% (up to seven steps). If even 40% is too big, it reduces the dimensions in proportion to the overshoot and searches again. The highest quality that fits is kept.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metadata">8. Metadata</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Re-encoded outputs</strong> (compressor, resizer, converters, cropper, rotate) are written from pixels only and contain no EXIF, GPS, XMP or IPTC metadata.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove EXIF</strong> works losslessly: for JPG it removes APP1 (EXIF/XMP), APP3–APP13 (e.g. IPTC), APP15, comments, the multi-picture index and any data after the end-of-image marker, while keeping JFIF, Adobe colour-transform data and (optionally) the ICC profile and a minimal orientation tag. For PNG it removes text, EXIF and time chunks; for WebP the EXIF and XMP chunks, updating the container header.</li>
    <li><strong>Colour:</strong> re-encoded images are in the sRGB colour space. Wide-gamut photos (Display P3) are converted, which can slightly reduce saturation on wide-gamut screens.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">9. Device limits</h2>
  <p>
    Browsers limit canvas size. On iPhone and iPad (WebKit), a single canvas may not exceed about 16.7 megapixels, so larger images are scaled down to that size and a note tells you so. On desktop browsers the limit used is {limits.maxCanvasPixelsDesktop / 1e6} megapixels, and {limits.maxCanvasPixelsLowMemory / 1e6} megapixels on devices that report 4 GB of RAM or less. Lossy PNG colour reduction is skipped above {limits.maxQuantizePixels / 1e6} megapixels (a note is shown). If a device runs out of memory, the tool shows an error instead of producing a broken file.
  </p>

  <h2 id="testing">10. Testing</h2>
  <p>
    The core logic — format detection, file-name sanitising, metadata removal, target-size search and page layout — is covered by automated unit tests, including checks that metadata removal leaves pixel data bit-for-bit identical. The tools are also tested end-to-end in a real browser engine (desktop and mobile viewport sizes) with normal, empty, oversized, corrupted, spoofed and oddly named files.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-source">11. Open-source components</h2>
  <p>Full licence texts and source links: <a href="/third-party-licenses.txt">third-party-licenses.txt</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/Hopding/pdf-lib" rel="noopener">pdf-lib</a> (MIT) — PDF creation</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/jamsinclair/jSquash" rel="noopener">jSquash / libwebp</a> (Apache-2.0 / BSD) — WebP encoding</li>
    {features.heicDecoder && (<li><a href="https://github.com/hoppergee/heic-to" rel="noopener">heic-to</a> / <a href="https://github.com/strukturag/libheif" rel="noopener">libheif</a> (LGPL-3.0) — HEIC decoding, served as a separate file built from the unmodified library</li>)}
    <li><a href="https://github.com/MikeKovarik/exifr" rel="noopener">exifr</a> (MIT) — metadata reading</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/photopea/UPNG.js" rel="noopener">UPNG.js</a> (MIT) — PNG quantization</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/101arrowz/fflate" rel="noopener">fflate</a> (MIT) — ZIP files</li>
    <li><a href="https://preactjs.com/" rel="noopener">Preact</a> (MIT) and <a href="https://astro.build/" rel="noopener">Astro</a> (MIT) — user interface and site generation</li>
  </ul>
</PageLayout>
